{
  "abstract": "Let $S,S'$ be very general K3 surfaces of degrees $2s^2d$ and $2d$ ($s\\ge2$) whose transcendental lattices are rationally but not integrally Hodge isometric, as in rStation paper 132. The integral Hodge classes on $S\\times S'$ in $H^2\\otimes H^2$ are spanned by $h\\otimes h'$ and a cross class $\\varphi$ with $\\varphi|_T=s\\iota$; paper 132 showed that the integral Hodge conjecture (IHC) on $S\\times S'^b$, $b\\le19$, is equivalent to the algebraicity of $\\varphi$, and asked whether $\\varphi$ and certain triple classes $\\xi_p$ on $S\\times S\\times S'$ are algebraic. We prove that $\\varphi$ is algebraic, using a twisted derived equivalence $D^b(S)\\simeq D^b(S',\\alpha')$ with $\\alpha'$ of order $s$ and untwisting the kernel by a twisted sheaf: the Brauer order lands exactly on $\\varphi$. Hence IHC holds on $S\\times S'^b$ for $b\\le19$. The third Chern character of $P_{13}\\otimes P_{23}^\\vee$ shows that $2\\xi_s$ is algebraic and $\\xi_s$ is algebraic for odd $s$; so IHC holds on $S\\times S\\times S'$ for odd $s$, and for even $s$ the cokernel is at most $\\mathbb Z/2$. Exact Smith-normal-form computations in the all-$H^2$ Künneth component ($d=1$; $s=2,3$; for $S^4\\times S'$ in a smaller model lattice) show that these classes saturate the integral Hodge lattice there on $S^3\\times S'$ and $S^4\\times S'$; for even $s$ this is a lattice statement, not algebraicity. On $S^2\\times S'^2$ ($d=1$, $s=2,3,5,7$) a new integral class $\\eta$ appears, and two natural families of kernel products do not reach it. We explain this by an index gap: $\\alpha'\\boxtimes\\alpha'$ on $S'\\times S'$ has period $s$ and index $s^2$. For $s=2$, Hotchkiss's method then gives a Severi–Brauer sevenfold over $S'\\times S'$ on which IHC fails in degree 6. For $\\gcd(s,6)=1$, a Newton identity for $6\\,\\mathrm{ch}_4$ shows that $\\eta$ is algebraic if Perry's categorical integral Hodge conjecture holds for $D^b(S^2\\times S'^2)$. Whether $\\eta$ is algebraic is left open.",
